%YAML 1.1
%TAG !u! tag:unity3d.com,2011:
--- !u!114 &11400000
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 7c7c947f0ccabb24dbf991df8e9f6d4f, type: 3}
  m_Name: pre experimental
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  defaultQuestions:
  - QuestionText: What is the defining characteristic of pre-experimental designs?
    Options:
    - They have a high degree of internal validity.
    - They lack a control group and/or random assignment.
    - They use complex statistical analysis.
    - They are conducted in laboratory settings only.
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: pre experimental
  - QuestionText: 'Pre-experimental designs are primarily used for:'
    Options:
    - Establishing cause-and-effect relationships conclusively.
    - Exploratory research and pilot studies to get a preliminary feel for the data.
    - Large-scale, funded research projects.
    - Testing well-established theories.
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: pre experimental
  - QuestionText: 'The "One-Shot Case Study" design can be represented as:'
    Options:
    - O1 X O2
    - X O
    - O1 O2
    - R X O
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: pre experimental
  - QuestionText: 'The major threat to internal validity in the "One-Shot Case Study"
      design is:'
    Options:
    - Maturation
    - The complete lack of a baseline for comparison; we don't know the pre-treatment
      state.
    - Testing
    - Selection
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: pre experimental
  - QuestionText: 'The "One-Group Pretest-Posttest Design" is represented as:'
    Options:
    - X O
    - O1 X O2
    - O1 O2
    - R X O
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: pre experimental
  - QuestionText: In the One-Group Pretest-Posttest design, a change from O1 to O2
      can be attributed to which of the following threats to internal validity?
    Options:
    - History, Maturation, Testing, Instrumentation, etc.
    - Only the treatment (X).
    - Random assignment.
    - The control group.
    CorrectOptionIndex: 0
    LevelName: pre experimental
  - QuestionText: 'The "Static Group Comparison" design involves:'
    Options:
    - One group measured once.
    - One group measured before and after a treatment.
    - Two groups, one with treatment and one without, but no random assignment.
    - Two randomly assigned groups.
    CorrectOptionIndex: 2
    LevelName: pre experimental
  - QuestionText: 'The key weakness of the "Static Group Comparison" design is:'
    Options:
    - Lack of a pre-test
    - Selection bias, because the groups may not be equivalent at the start.
    - The cost is too high.
    - It has too many groups.
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: pre experimental
  - QuestionText: 'The main advantage of pre-experimental designs is their:'
    Options:
    - High internal validity
    - Practicality, ease, and convenience to conduct in real-world settings.
    - Ability to control all variables
    - Complex design
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: pre experimental
  - QuestionText: 'Pre-experimental designs are also known as:'
    Options:
    - True experimental designs
    - Quasi-experimental designs
    - Weak experimental designs
    - Factorial designs
    CorrectOptionIndex: 2
    LevelName: pre experimental
  - QuestionText: In which design is it impossible to determine if the group improved
      because there is no baseline measure?
    Options:
    - One-Group Pretest-Posttest
    - Static Group Comparison
    - One-Shot Case Study
    - Solomon Four-Group
    CorrectOptionIndex: 2
    LevelName: pre experimental
  - QuestionText: The threat of "maturation" is most relevant to which pre-experimental
      design?
    Options:
    - One-Shot Case Study
    - One-Group Pretest-Posttest
    - Static Group Comparison
    - All of the above
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: pre experimental
  - QuestionText: 'If subjects become tired or bored between O1 and O2, it threatens
      validity in the One-Group Pretest-Posttest design. This is called:'
    Options:
    - History
    - Maturation
    - Testing
    - Mortality
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: pre experimental
  - QuestionText: 'The threat of "history" refers to:'
    Options:
    - Events external to the experiment that occur between the pre-test and post-test.
    - The natural aging process of subjects.
    - The effect of taking a test on the scores of a second testing.
    - Changes in the measurement instrument.
    CorrectOptionIndex: 0
    LevelName: pre experimental
  - QuestionText: 'The most significant limitation of all pre-experimental designs
      is their:'
    Options:
    - High cost
    - Low internal validity
    - Inability to be conducted in a school
    - Requirement for specialized equipment
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: pre experimental
  - QuestionText: Why is the "One-Shot Case Study" considered the weakest of all
      pre-experimental designs?
    Options:
    - It has two groups.
    - It provides no basis for comparison, neither with a pre-test nor a control
      group.
    - It is too expensive.
    - It uses random assignment.
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: pre experimental
  - QuestionText: How does the "One-Group Pretest-Posttest" design improve upon the
      "One-Shot Case Study"?
    Options:
    - By adding a control group.
    - By providing a baseline (pre-test) against which to compare the post-test results.
    - By using random assignment.
    - By eliminating the history threat.
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: pre experimental
  - QuestionText: Despite having a pre-test, why is the "One-Group Pretest-Posttest"
      design still weak?
    Options:
    - It is too long.
    - Because any change from O1 to O2 could be due to the treatment OR to other
      factors like history, maturation, or testing.
    - It lacks a post-test.
    - It has too many participants.
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: pre experimental
  - QuestionText: 'In the "Static Group Comparison," the difference between the post-tests
      of the two groups could be due to:'
    Options:
    - The treatment only.
    - Pre-existing differences between the groups (selection bias) rather than the
      treatment.
    - Random assignment.
    - The fact that both groups took a pre-test.
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: pre experimental
  - QuestionText: 'A teacher uses a new teaching method and then gives a test. This
      is an example of a:'
    Options:
    - One-Group Pretest-Posttest Design
    - Static Group Comparison Design
    - One-Shot Case Study Design
    - True Experiment
    CorrectOptionIndex: 2
    LevelName: pre experimental
  - QuestionText: 'A teacher gives a pre-test, uses a new teaching method, and then
      gives a post-test. This is a:'
    Options:
    - One-Shot Case Study Design
    - One-Group Pretest-Posttest Design
    - Static Group Comparison Design
    - Quasi-Experiment
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: pre experimental
  - QuestionText: 'A researcher compares the final exam scores of students who chose
      to attend extra tutoring sessions with those who did not. This is a:'
    Options:
    - One-Shot Case Study
    - One-Group Pretest-Posttest
    - Static Group Comparison
    - True Experiment
    CorrectOptionIndex: 2
    LevelName: pre experimental
  - QuestionText: What is the primary threat to validity in the "Static Group Comparison"
      design?
    Options:
    - Testing
    - Maturation
    - Selection
    - History
    CorrectOptionIndex: 2
    LevelName: pre experimental
  - QuestionText: 'The term "internal validity" refers to:'
    Options:
    - The ability to generalize findings to other settings.
    - The extent to which we can be confident that the independent variable caused
      the dependent variable.
    - The consistency of the measurement.
    - The truthfulness of the literature review.
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: pre experimental
  - QuestionText: Which of the following is a correct representation of the Static
      Group Comparison design?
    Options:
    - O1 X O2
    - X O1 --- O2
    - O1 O2
    - R X O
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: pre experimental
  - QuestionText: "Assertion (A):\_Pre-experimental designs are a valid method for
      establishing cause-and-effect.\_Reason (R):\_They systematically control for
      all major threats to internal validity."
    Options:
    - Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.
    - Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A.
    - A is true but R is false.
    - A is false but R is true.
    CorrectOptionIndex: 3
    LevelName: pre experimental
  - QuestionText: 'A company shows an inspirational video to employees and then measures
      their motivation. They find high motivation and conclude the video worked.
      The flaw in this conclusion, from a design perspective, is that it uses a:'
    Options:
    - One-Group Pretest-Posttest Design
    - Static Group Comparison Design
    - One-Shot Case Study Design
    - True Experimental Design
    CorrectOptionIndex: 2
    LevelName: pre experimental
  - QuestionText: Contrast a "One-Group Pretest-Posttest" design with a "True Experimental"
      design (like Pretest-Posttest Control Group Design).
    Options:
    - They are identical.
    - The true experimental design adds random assignment to a control group, which
      controls for internal validity threats.
    - The pre-experimental design is more complex.
    - The true experimental design has no pre-test.
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: pre experimental
  - QuestionText: 'If participants in a One-Group Pretest-Posttest study drop out
      before the post-test, it threatens validity through:'
    Options:
    - Mortality/Attrition
    - History
    - Testing
    - Regression
    CorrectOptionIndex: 0
    LevelName: pre experimental
  - QuestionText: 'The "Hawthorne Effect" is a potential threat in pre-experimental
      designs because:'
    Options:
    - The control group might be jealous.
    - Participants may change their behavior simply because they know they are in
      an experiment, not because of the treatment.
    - The researcher might be biased.
    - The instruments are faulty.
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: pre experimental
  - QuestionText: Which of the following is the STRONGEST pre-experimental design?
    Options:
    - One-Shot Case Study
    - One-Group Pretest-Posttest
    - Static Group Comparison
    - They are all equally weak.
    CorrectOptionIndex: 2
    LevelName: pre experimental
  - QuestionText: 'A flaw in interpreting results from a One-Group Pretest-Posttest
      design is to:'
    Options:
    - Attribute any change solely to the treatment without considering rival explanations.
    - Use a control group.
    - Use random assignment.
    - Conduct a pilot study.
    CorrectOptionIndex: 0
    LevelName: pre experimental
  - QuestionText: 'The term "external validity" refers to generalizability. Pre-experimental
      designs:'
    Options:
    - Have high external validity.
    - Often have good external validity to the specific setting where they are conducted
      because they are so naturalistic.
    - Have no external validity.
    - Are only generalizable to labs.
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: pre experimental
  - QuestionText: The "Placebo Effect" is a concern in which pre-experimental design?
    Options:
    - One-Shot Case Study
    - One-Group Pretest-Posttest
    - Static Group Comparison
    - All of the above
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: pre experimental
  - QuestionText: 'Pre-experimental designs are most appropriate when:'
    Options:
    - A definitive causal conclusion is required.
    - The research is exploratory, and the goal is to identify promising variables
      for future, more rigorous study.
    - Funding is unlimited.
    - The population is very large.
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: pre experimental
  - QuestionText: 'The statistical analysis for a One-Group Pretest-Posttest design
      often involves a:'
    Options:
    - Independent samples t-test
    - Paired-samples t-test (or dependent t-test)
    - One-way ANOVA
    - Correlation
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: pre experimental
  - QuestionText: 'In a Static Group Comparison, the appropriate statistical test
      would be a(n):'
    Options:
    - Paired-samples t-test
    - Independent samples t-test
    - Chi-square test
    - Pearson's r
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: pre experimental
  - QuestionText: 'The concept of "ecological validity" is often high in pre-experimental
      designs because they:'
    Options:
    - Are conducted in artificial labs.
    - Are often conducted in natural, real-world environments.
    - Use highly controlled procedures.
    - Have many groups.
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: pre experimental
  - QuestionText: 'A researcher should clearly state the use of a pre-experimental
      design in the "Limitations" section to:'
    Options:
    - Hide the weakness of the study.
    - Acknowledge the low internal validity and caution readers against making strong
      causal inferences.
    - Make the report longer.
    - Prove their statistical skills.
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: pre experimental
  - QuestionText: 'The step that can be added to a pre-experimental design to strengthen
      it slightly is:'
    Options:
    - Random Assignment
    - Adding a carefully selected comparison group
    - Removing the pre-test
    - Ignoring threats to validity
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: pre experimental
  - QuestionText: 'The threat of "instrumentation" is relevant when:'
    Options:
    - The measuring tool changes between the pre-test and post-test.
    - Participants mature.
    - An external event occurs.
    - Participants drop out.
    CorrectOptionIndex: 0
    LevelName: pre experimental
  - QuestionText: 'The "One-Group Pretest-Posttest" design is also sometimes called
      a:'
    Options:
    - Static Group Comparison
    - Before-After Design
    - Cross-Sectional Design
    - Longitudinal Design
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: pre experimental
  - QuestionText: 'The fundamental question that a pre-experimental design cannot
      answer definitively is:'
    Options:
    - What is the mean score?
    - Did the treatment cause the change?
    - How many participants were there?
    - What was the research question?
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: pre experimental
  - QuestionText: 'If a pre-test influences the scores on the post-test (e.g., through
      practice), this is the threat of:'
    Options:
    - History
    - Testing
    - Maturation
    - Regression
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: pre experimental
  - QuestionText: 'The term "confounding variable" is best described as a variable
      that:'
    Options:
    - Is the main independent variable.
    - Is the dependent variable.
    - Varies systematically with the independent variable and could be the true cause
      of the effect.
    - Is kept constant.
    CorrectOptionIndex: 2
    LevelName: pre experimental
  - QuestionText: 'The logical structure of pre-experimental designs is often:'
    Options:
    - Post hoc, ergo propter hoc (after this, therefore because of this).
    - Correlational.
    - Deductive.
    - Inductive.
    CorrectOptionIndex: 0
    LevelName: pre experimental
  - QuestionText: 'A key ethical advantage of some pre-experimental designs is that
      they:'
    Options:
    - Always require deception.
    - Can be used when it is unethical to withhold a treatment from a control group.
    - Guarantee beneficial results for participants.
    - Are the most rigorous.
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: pre experimental
  - QuestionText: 'The recommended next step after conducting a study with a pre-experimental
      design is usually to:'
    Options:
    - Publish the results as conclusive evidence.
    - Conduct a true experiment or a strong quasi-experiment to verify the findings.
    - Abandon the research area.
    - Change the hypothesis.
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: pre experimental
  - QuestionText: 'In the hierarchy of evidence for intervention studies, pre-experimental
      designs are ranked:'
    Options:
    - At the top, as the most reliable.
    - At the bottom, as the least reliable.
    - In the middle.
    - The same as randomized controlled trials.
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: pre experimental
  - QuestionText: 'The most critical takeaway for a researcher using a pre-experimental
      design is to:'
    Options:
    - Overstate their conclusions.
    - Interpret the results with extreme caution and avoid definitive causal statements.
    - Ignore all limitations.
    - Focus only on the statistical significance.
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: pre experimental
